I added `specdojo exec queue`. It saves a reserved order of grade runs, `exec run` and `post-integration build-dist` to a file and runs the steps in that order. The new unit tests pass: 15 tests, and 58 together with the related existing ones. I haven't run `npm run typecheck`, `lint:ts`, `test:unit`, `lint:md` or the other parent checks myself. The runner runs those after this and its results are the ones that count. Nothing has run the CLI end to end on real child processes yet, either by hand or in an integration test. I only checked `--help`, `status`, and that an unallowed step is rejected.

**How it works**
- **Commands:** `exec queue add <queue-id> --step <spec> / --step-continue <spec>` creates a queue. `run <queue-id>` runs or resumes it, `status [queue-id]` shows it, and `cancel <queue-id> --msg` cancels it.
- **Where it's kept:** `<execution_path>/queues/<queue-id>.json`, under git, so it survives the orchestrator session. It records each step's state, times and exit code, plus the step and reason where a queue stopped. Unfinished queues also appear at the end of `exec status`, under `queues:`.
- **Allowed steps:** only `grade run ...`, `exec run ...` and `post-integration build-dist`. `exec run` needs `--register`, `--register-filter`, `--auto` or `--task`, and `--job` and `--plan` are rejected. Arguments are split on whitespace only and passed to the CLI directly, so no shell is involved. Quotes and backslashes are rejected.
- **Waiting and failure:** each step starts only after the previous child process exits; nothing searches for processes by name. A `--step` failure stops the queue; a `--step-continue` failure moves on. Running a stopped queue again restarts at the failed step and doesn't repeat finished ones.
- **Build step:** `post-integration build-dist` waits for the exec lock and then runs `npm run build`. A failed build leaves the same failure marker as PJR-8KXJ does after integration.

**Decisions to check**
- **Why a new command:** Job `task.steps` runs arbitrary shell commands, which the ticket rules out, and its retries start from the first step. Adding reservations to `exec run` would have made lock handling messy, since that command holds the exec lock. The full comparison is in the ticket's results section.
- **Other runs can slip in:** the queue doesn't hold the exec lock between steps, so another run can start between two queue steps. Holding it would make the queue's own `exec run` steps wait forever.
- **Exit code:** `exec queue run` exits 0 only when every step succeeded. It exits 1 when stopped, cancelled, or when a `--step-continue` step failed.
- **Commit:** the queue file isn't committed automatically. You or the orchestrator commit it, the same way as the register record commits.

**Files changed**
- New: `src/exec/queue.ts`, `src/exec/queue-command.ts`, `tests/src/exec/queue.test.ts`.
- Modified: `src/exec/commands.ts`, and `src/exec/post-integration.ts`, where I moved the failure-marker logic out into `updateDistBuildFailureMarker` so both paths share it.
- Docs: `command-reference.md` (new section "実行の予約（exec queue）"), `exec-operation-guide.md`, `sysd-job-execution.md`.
- The PJR-1565 ticket's work table and results section are filled in, with proposed `CHANGELOG.md` wording.

**Needs your approval:** proposed wording for `.agents/specdojo-orchestrator.agent.md`, which is protected, and for the prj-0001 playbook is in the ticket's results section. I edited neither; apply them on a feature branch if you agree. I didn't change any other protected settings.

Other checks: Prettier, the history-link check, the source-import check and catalog validation all passed. ESLint on the changed files gave only the existing function-length warning in `commands.ts`.

